 (action: 0), showing no aggression pre-flop.\n\n**Key considerations:**\n1. **Hand Strength**: Pocket Jacks are strong but vulnerable to overcards (Q, K, A) on the flop\n2. **Position**: I'm likely in the big blind since opponent acted first with a call\n3. **Opponent's Action**: A limp (just calling) often indicates a weaker hand, speculative hand (small pairs, suited connectors), or a trap with a monster\n4. **Stack Sizes**: Both roughly even at ~20,000 chips\n5. **Tournament Context**: Early in the match (hand 4/20), so no need for desperate moves\n\n**Promising moves:**\n- **Check (0)**: See a free flop, but gives opponent a free card to potentially outdraw me\n- **Raise 200-400**: Standard raise to build the pot and get information; opponent might fold weak hands\n- **Raise 600-800**: Larger raise to apply more pressure and protect against draws\n- **All-in (-2)**: Too aggressive for this situation; risks entire stack on hand 4\n\nWith JJ, I want to build the pot while protecting my hand. A check risks allowing weak hands to see free cards. A moderate raise (3-4x the big blind) is standard to:\n- Build pot value with my strong hand\n- Force opponent to pay to see the flop\n- Potentially win immediately if they fold\n- Get information about their hand strength\n\nReason: Pocket Jacks warrant aggression pre-flop to protect against overcards and build value. A raise of 600 chips (assuming 100/200 blinds) gives me fold equity while building the pot when called by weaker hands. This is superior to checking, which gives free cards, and superior to going all-in, which is unnecessarily risky this early.\n\nAnswer: 600"}
